      title: `Postjunctional Nicotinic Acetylcholine Receptors (nAChRs)`,
      content: `Postjunctional nicotinic acetylcholine receptors belong to a large pentameric family of ligand-gated ion channels that includes the **_5-HT3_** , glycine, and **_γ_** -aminobutyric acid ( **_GABA_** ) receptors. These complexes are synthesized inside muscle cells and anchored explicitly to the end-plate membrane matrix by a specialized anchoring protein matrix called **rapsyn** . Receptor structure shifts between two distinct populations during baseline developmental maturation: 

- **Adult (Mature) Receptors:** Structurally localized at the postsynaptic membrane interface and organized configurationally as a pentameric complex consisting of **_α2βδε_** subunits. Organizationally, the pentamer exhibits a strict sequential layout written as **_αεαδβ_** . 

- **Fetal (Immature) Receptors:** Expressed configurationally as **_αβγδ_** subunits, where a low-conductance **_γ_** subunit replaces the mature adult **_ε_** subunit. The functional development of motor axon innervation during the initial baseline weeks of life drives the normal replacement of the fetal **_γ_** subunit with the adult **_ε_** subunit. 

Each of the two **_α_** subunits contains an explicit ligand-binding pocket for acetylcholine (ACh) situated inside specialized protein folds roughly 3.0 nm above the muscle membrane surface. These sites are localized precisely at the interfaces of the **_αH-ε_** and **_αL-δ_** subunits. Once activated by agonist binding, the mature adult receptor channel exhibits a significantly shorter open-channel duration and higher unit conductance to sodium ( **_Na_**<sup>**_+_**</sup> ), potassium ( **_K_**<sup>**_+_**</sup> ), and calcium ( **_Ca_**<sup>**_2+_**</sup> ) ions compared to the fetal variant. The fetal receptor features significantly smaller channel conductance and prolonged open-channel durations.`
