      content: `Tracheal extubation should not be performed until complete recovery is confirmed. Complete recovery is defined objectively as an adductor pollicis TOF ratio $\\ge 0.90$ via electromyography or mechanomyography, or a ratio $> 1.0$ via unnormalized acceleromyography. Visual or tactile assessment cannot reliably identify residual paralysis. Clinicians are unable to subjectively detect fade when the true TOF ratio exceeds 0.30 to 0.40. Ratios under 0.90 are associated with airway obstruction, impaired pharyngeal function, increased risk of silent gastric aspiration, attenuated hypoxic ventilatory drive, and significant muscle weakness.`
